1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,679
Yassou — this is Liam, in for Bear.
Today's ask is the oldest question in

2
00:00:04,679 --> 00:00:08,734
philosophy, wearing brand-new clothes: how
do you know what's true, when a machine

3
00:00:08,734 --> 00:00:12,165
can generate a million plausible claims a
minute? Philosophers called this

4
00:00:12,165 --> 00:00:16,220
epistemology. Today we rebuild it as
software — because checking claims by hand

5
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:17,780
stopped scaling a while ago.

6
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:22,782
Act one. The problem isn't that people
lie. It's that the volume of claims outran

7
00:00:22,782 --> 00:00:24,450
the apparatus for checking them.

8
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:28,698
For centuries, this apparatus lived in
rooms like this one. Trace the
justification.

9
00:00:28,698 --> 00:00:33,599
Check the source. Test the coherence.
Slow, careful — and built for a world
where

10
00:00:33,599 --> 00:00:35,560
claims arrived one at a time.

11
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:41,141
Now claims arrive as a firehose — text,
images, video, much of it synthetic, some
of

12
00:00:41,141 --> 00:00:45,676
it adversarial. One human, applying the
classical checks by hand, against that.
The

13
00:00:45,676 --> 00:00:47,420
mismatch is the whole story.

14
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:51,178
And it's really two different problems
wearing one coat. First: I generated this

15
00:00:51,178 --> 00:00:55,804
with a model — is it true? Second: the
feed is saturated with synthetic claims —

16
00:00:55,804 --> 00:01:00,140
what do I let in? Conflate them and every
fix lands on the wrong problem.

17
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:06,160
Act two. What knowledge is — and the tiny
1963 paper that broke the definition.

18
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:10,404
The classical definition stacked three
conditions. You know something if it's
true,

19
00:01:10,404 --> 00:01:15,002
you believe it, and you're justified in
believing it. Justified, true, belief. For

20
00:01:15,002 --> 00:01:16,770
two thousand years, that held.

21
00:01:16,770 --> 00:01:20,575
Then Edmund Gettier published three pages
that wrecked it. His counterexamples

22
00:01:20,575 --> 00:01:25,763
showed a belief can be justified, and true
— and still not knowledge, because the

23
00:01:25,763 --> 00:01:28,530
justification and the truth connect only
by accident.

24
00:01:28,530 --> 00:01:33,347
Here's the machine version. A model cites
a source that doesn't exist — but the

25
00:01:33,347 --> 00:01:37,521
number happens to be right. Correct
answer, broken path. It looks identical to

26
00:01:37,521 --> 00:01:41,374
knowledge, and the same process fails
silently next time. That's not knowledge.

27
00:01:41,374 --> 00:01:42,980
That's luck wearing its costume.

28
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:48,598
So the fix isn't asking whether the answer
is true. Watch — we ask Claude to check

29
00:01:48,598 --> 00:01:50,250
whether the justification is load-bearing.

30
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:54,858
Two separate scores. One for truth against
a retrieved source. One for entailment —

31
00:01:54,858 --> 00:01:59,136
does the stated reasoning actually support
the claim. High truth with low entailment

32
00:01:59,136 --> 00:02:03,744
doesn't pass: it gets flagged as Gettier
risk. Most fact-checkers only run the
first

33
00:02:03,744 --> 00:02:05,060
score. That's the hole.

34
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:10,345
You can put a number on the danger. Take
the truth score, multiply by one minus the

35
00:02:10,345 --> 00:02:14,698
entailment score. High truth, low
entailment — the product spikes. That
spike is the

36
00:02:14,698 --> 00:02:18,740
claim most likely to burn you: right
answer, invisible to a truth-only checker.

37
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:23,244
And here's what the equation commits you
to: truth and justification stay separate

38
00:02:23,244 --> 00:02:27,402
axes, forever. Blend them into one
confidence score and the dangerous case

39
00:02:27,402 --> 00:02:30,520
disappears back into the average. Back to
the pipeline.

40
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:35,395
Act three. Where does justification have
to live — inside the mind, or in the track

41
00:02:35,395 --> 00:02:35,700
record?

42
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:39,905
Philosophy split on this. Internalists
said justification must be visible to the

43
00:02:39,905 --> 00:02:44,460
believer — you can produce your reasons.
Show your work, from the inside.

44
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:48,066
Externalists said something colder: what
matters is whether the process that

45
00:02:48,066 --> 00:02:53,639
produced the belief is reliable — whether
it has a track record — even if nobody can

46
00:02:53,639 --> 00:02:54,950
articulate why it works.

47
00:02:54,950 --> 00:03:00,710
Here's why the cold answer wins for
machines. Ask a model to explain itself
and it

48
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:05,390
produces a fluent justification — which
often isn't the process that made the

49
00:03:05,390 --> 00:03:09,350
answer. Interpretability work keeps
finding that gap. Self-report is an
internalist

50
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:10,430
witness that confabulates.

51
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:15,014
So the architecture is reliabilist at the
spine: log every process's outcomes, and

52
00:03:15,014 --> 00:03:20,303
let the empirical record gate the trust. A
model that says ninety percent, but lands

53
00:03:20,303 --> 00:03:24,887
sixty in that bucket, gets treated as a
sixty. Calibration is just honesty,

54
00:03:24,887 --> 00:03:25,240
measured.

55
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:30,009
In code it's almost embarrassingly small.
A dictionary of track records. Under ten

56
00:03:30,009 --> 00:03:35,145
trials, no verdict — insufficient record.
A confident model with a bad history is

57
00:03:35,145 --> 00:03:39,180
still not trusted. The self-report gets
demoted to a debugging signal.

58
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:44,480
Act four. Every justification needs a
justification. What stops the falling?

59
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:49,576
Believe A because of B, B because of C, C
because of… this is the regress problem,

60
00:03:49,576 --> 00:03:53,473
and philosophy offered two exits. Hit
bedrock — a basic belief that justifies

61
00:03:53,473 --> 00:03:57,970
itself. Or deny the bedrock, and let
beliefs hold each other up in a web.

62
00:03:57,970 --> 00:04:01,342
Retrieval-augmented generation is
foundationalism with a product name: stop
the

63
00:04:01,342 --> 00:04:07,075
regress at a document you can point to.
And its known failure is the classical one
—

64
00:04:07,075 --> 00:04:12,133
the bedrock is only as good as the rock. A
wrong source, confidently cited, grounds

65
00:04:12,133 --> 00:04:12,470
nothing.

66
00:04:12,470 --> 00:04:17,678
The web has its own classic failure. A
conspiracy wall is perfectly coherent —
every

67
00:04:17,678 --> 00:04:21,498
thread supports every other thread.
Coherence measures how well beliefs fit

68
00:04:21,498 --> 00:04:24,970
together. It cannot tell you the whole
structure isn't floating.

69
00:04:24,970 --> 00:04:29,673
Now the machine version of the floating
web. Ask three models, get three
agreements,

70
00:04:29,673 --> 00:04:34,041
call it confirmation. But if they trained
on overlapping data, that's not three

71
00:04:34,041 --> 00:04:38,072
witnesses — it's one witness talking to
itself three times. Correlated error,

72
00:04:38,072 --> 00:04:39,080
dressed as consensus.

73
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:43,656
So the honest architecture uses both,
discounted. A foundationalist anchor — one

74
00:04:43,656 --> 00:04:48,614
real, checkable source. A coherentist
cross-check — does it fit what you already

75
00:04:48,614 --> 00:04:53,572
trust — with agreement explicitly
discounted by how much the sources share
ancestry.

76
00:04:53,572 --> 00:04:55,860
Correlated agreement adds uncertainty, not
confidence.

77
00:04:55,860 --> 00:05:00,267
Formally, the shared training lineage is a
hidden common cause behind the agreeing

78
00:05:00,267 --> 00:05:05,353
sources — and you model it as one, instead
of pretending your witnesses never met.

79
00:05:05,353 --> 00:05:09,421
Ignore it, and your confidence inflates
exactly when the sources are least

80
00:05:09,421 --> 00:05:09,760
independent.

81
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:13,980
Act five. The thought experiment that
stopped being a thought experiment.

82
00:05:13,980 --> 00:05:18,809
Descartes imagined a demon powerful enough
to fake your entire experience — and

83
00:05:18,809 --> 00:05:23,639
asked how you'd ever know. For four
centuries that was philosophy's most
famous

84
00:05:23,639 --> 00:05:24,010
hypothetical.

85
00:05:24,010 --> 00:05:28,153
The demon shipped. Text, images, video —
synthesized at scale, cheap, sometimes

86
00:05:28,153 --> 00:05:32,987
tuned to deceive. The question 'how would
you know if your inputs were manufactured'

87
00:05:32,987 --> 00:05:36,440
now has a real adversary and a real cost
function.

88
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:41,162
Hume's problem rides along too: past
regularity never guaranteed the future. A

89
00:05:41,162 --> 00:05:46,277
model's track record holds until the
distribution shifts — new domain, new
adversary

90
00:05:46,277 --> 00:05:50,606
— and then yesterday's reliability quietly
stops describing today. The induction

91
00:05:50,606 --> 00:05:52,180
problem, running in production.

92
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:56,825
Nobody ever refuted the skeptic. Hume's
own move was better: proceed on fallible,

93
00:05:56,825 --> 00:06:01,112
revisable grounds, and build habits that
catch errors over time. For synthetic

94
00:06:01,112 --> 00:06:06,114
media: don't chase perfect fake-detection
— an arms race you don't win. Build chains

95
00:06:06,114 --> 00:06:07,900
of custody, and cheap corrections.

96
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:12,163
One rule worth tattooing somewhere: a
claim's pull on your belief never exceeds
the

97
00:06:12,163 --> 00:06:17,036
trust you can verify in its channel. Cap
it at the minimum of provenance and
content

98
00:06:17,036 --> 00:06:20,690
— because confident style is cheap to
fake, and provenance is not.

99
00:06:20,690 --> 00:06:26,268
Act six. The one place where old
epistemology and working code were always
the same

100
00:06:26,268 --> 00:06:26,640
subject.

101
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:31,057
Bayesian epistemology says belief isn't
binary — it comes in degrees, and

102
00:06:31,057 --> 00:06:35,474
rationality is updating those degrees by
the likelihood of the evidence.
Calibration

103
00:06:35,474 --> 00:06:40,260
curves and Bayesian updating in machine
learning aren't inspired by this. They are

104
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:42,100
this, in a different department.

105
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:45,945
The pipeline consequence: your independent
checks — retrieval match, entailment,

106
00:06:45,945 --> 00:06:50,559
discounted consensus, track record —
combine as sequential updates on one
credence,

107
00:06:50,559 --> 00:06:56,711
not as a majority vote. A vote throws away
exactly what you know about each check's

108
00:06:56,711 --> 00:06:57,480
differential reliability.

109
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:02,237
Surprise sets the revision speed. Evidence
you already expected barely moves you. A

110
00:07:02,237 --> 00:07:06,262
genuinely surprising observation — low
probability under your current belief —

111
00:07:06,262 --> 00:07:11,385
should move you a lot, and trigger a re-
check of everything downstream. Negative
log

112
00:07:11,385 --> 00:07:13,580
probability: the math of paying attention.

113
00:07:13,580 --> 00:07:18,391
The end of the pipe is a decision rule.
Set the threshold from the cost of being

114
00:07:18,391 --> 00:07:22,635
wrong — clinical high, trivia low. Above
it, act. Below it, say the honest thing:

115
00:07:22,635 --> 00:07:26,880
not confident enough — escalate to a
human. Forcing an answer is the failure
mode.

116
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:30,857
Let's recap with Claude. Knowledge needs
the justification to be load-bearing, not

117
00:07:30,857 --> 00:07:35,828
just the answer to be right. Trust the
track record, not the self-report. Anchor
in

118
00:07:35,828 --> 00:07:39,473
a checkable source, and discount agreement
between sources that share ancestry.

119
00:07:39,473 --> 00:07:43,450
Don't chase certainty — build provenance
and cheap corrections. And act on

120
00:07:43,450 --> 00:07:45,770
calibrated credence, with the right to
abstain.

121
00:07:45,770 --> 00:07:50,155
Your turn. Here's the prompt: I want to
verify a claim the way a computational

122
00:07:50,155 --> 00:07:53,955
epistemologist would. Take this claim and
its cited source, and walk five stages

123
00:07:53,955 --> 00:07:58,048
explicitly — does the source actually
entail the claim, what track record would
I

124
00:07:58,048 --> 00:08:01,848
need to trust the process that produced
it, what independent anchor and what

125
00:08:01,848 --> 00:08:05,063
correlated agreement exist, what
provenance caps my trust, and what
calibrated

126
00:08:05,063 --> 00:08:10,033
credence with an abstain option do you
land on. Run it on a claim from your own

127
00:08:10,033 --> 00:08:14,418
field — and watch where truth and
justification come apart. That gap is the
whole

128
00:08:14,418 --> 00:08:14,710
episode.

129
00:08:14,710 --> 00:08:18,790
Knowing What's True in the Age of AI.
Liam, in for Bear.

